{"level":30,"time":1778521249693,"pid":86444,"hostname":"Williams-MacBook-Pro.local","step":"review-runs","item":"13.md","index":5,"runIndex":"run-2","type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001","id":"msg_018J8t1u2DJciCQSfsB8TCj6","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Good news on the parking/bollard check:\n1. Transformer Pad west of Building 1: 32.2 feet from parking/traffic (PASSES - exceeds 4 ft minimum, so no bollards required)\n2. Transformer Pad west of Building 2: 16 feet from parking/traffic (PASSES - exceeds 4 ft minimum)\n3. Transformer Pad west of Building 8: 31.2 feet from parking/traffic (PASSES - exceeds 4 ft minimum)\n4. Transformer Pad between Bldgs 4 & 5: 74.4 feet from parking/traffic (PASSES - exceeds 4 ft minimum)\n5. Transformer Pad between Bldgs 7 & 6: 85.3 feet from parking/traffic (PASSES - exceeds 4 ft minimum)\n\nSo none of the transformer pads are within 4 feet of parking areas, which means EL-13.38 is not applicable to these pads.\n\nHowever, I still need to address the issue from earlier about the hot-stick clearances. The measurements showed:\n- Pad west of Bldg 2: 9.3 feet (less than 10-foot requirement)\n- Pad between Bldgs 4 & 5: 5.1 feet (much less than 10-foot requirement)\n- Pad east of Bldg 7: 5.1 feet (much less than 10-foot requirement)\n\nBUT - the vision tool measured to the closest building wall, not necessarily the access door side. Without marked access door orientations, I cannot definitively say if these failures are real or not. This might be \"not-verifiable\" because access door orientations are not marked.\n\nHowever, I should be more careful here. The grouping guidance says that the access door side is where hot-stick operations happen. \n\nIf the access door faces toward open space rather than the building wall, then the 10-foot clearance requirement wouldn't apply to that wall. I need to verify the easement exhibits and check if transformer pads are on private property, then review grading, underground utilities, overhead lines, and fire lane markings.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":7,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1678,"cache_read_input_tokens":52065,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1678},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"64cdee63-31dd-4baa-841a-32fa24fe3ff6","uuid":"aba354a1-a6a0-4e86-8a75-aa32ab1f076a"}
