{"level":30,"time":1778521851402,"pid":86444,"hostname":"Williams-MacBook-Pro.local","step":"review-runs","item":"2.md","index":7,"runIndex":"run-3","type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001","id":"msg_01KNaadb3tBffiZNiAe2nijN","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The vision tool failed for Sheet 35. Let me try a different approach. I should read the plant schedule more carefully to see if I can find any mention of the mitigation tree species. Actually, let me search for information about which trees are being used for mitigation by looking at the complete plant schedule again or checking if there's a landscape notes sheet (Sheet 36 or 37).\n\nActually, wait. Looking back at what I read from Sheet 35 blocks.md, I see:\n- Block 11: SHADE TREES (Live Oak, Cedar Elm)\n- Block 17: ORNAMENTAL TREES (Texas Mountain Laurel, Mexican Redbud)\n- Block 14: SHRUBS\n\nBut the mitigation trees aren't explicitly identified with species in the blocks I read. They're just marked as \"M\" on the plan.\n\nThis could be a deficiency - if the mitigation trees are not identified by species on the landscape plan or plant schedule, then the applicant cannot verify whether they are utility-compatible for the restricted zones near the OHE line.\n\nLet me also check if there's additional information in the landscape details sheet (Sheet 37) or if there's a supplementary document that identifies these trees.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":7,"cache_creation_input_tokens":850,"cache_read_input_tokens":53407,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":850},"output_tokens":4,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"dedcba6e-469f-4ff1-8623-d3aaae962125","uuid":"8e641c47-3ec9-43c8-a011-110f8a3ff93b"}
