{"level":30,"time":1778523366785,"pid":86444,"hostname":"Williams-MacBook-Pro.local","step":"review-runs","item":"13.md","index":2,"runIndex":"run-1","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_011XCQCa7y9XDg8dGZPSfgcV","type":"tool_result","content":"/Users/wnavey/code/controlroom/conductor/workspace/projects/63cead15-41f8-418c-b0ef-bd5c2b44719a/supplementary-docs/master-comment-report/12-water-quality-review.md:* U1: Comment remains. A concrete splash pad may help with erosion but it does not help diffuse the flow. Where feasible the underdrain pipe should discharge to a gravel trench in order to diffuse the flow and promote infiltration and recharge. If a gravel trench is not feasible, other options are shown in the City of Austin Standards 508S-16 through 20. If there is existing gravel please show it on the pond sheets. \n/Users/wnavey/code/controlroom/conductor/workspace/projects/63cead15-41f8-418c-b0ef-bd5c2b44719a/supplementary-docs/master-comment-report/04-drainage-engineering-review.md:* U1: Comment pending. We still need an analysis of the existing channel on the site. If you are planning to discharge to it, we will need to ensure it has capacity. Please note that your outfall is within an electrical easement. You will need to provide approval from the easements’ owner to do this. Please note the intent of this code is also to cause no adverse impact to your neighbors.\n/Users/wnavey/code/controlroom/conductor/workspace/projects/63cead15-41f8-418c-b0ef-bd5c2b44719a/supplementary-docs/master-comment-report/04-drainage-engineering-review.md:[Figure: Cross-section detail of a detention pond structure showing a wall with TW 677.50, FG 672.00, and a 6\" PVC discharge pipe at 1.00% slope onto a 3' x 4' concrete splash pad]\n/Users/wnavey/code/controlroom/conductor/workspace/projects/63cead15-41f8-418c-b0ef-bd5c2b44719a/supplementary-docs/consolidated-site-plan-application/02-contacts-information.md:Provide all professional and/or trade contact information, if applicable. Examples include general contractor, electrical contractor, landscape architect, etc.\n/Users/wnavey/code/controlroom/conductor/workspace/projects/63cead15-41f8-418c-b0ef-bd5c2b44719a/supplementary-docs/consolidated-site-plan-application/04-application-assessment-and-site-area.md:Indicate the existing and proposed percent of impervious cover. Impervious cover is anything that prevents water from percolating into the ground, such as buildings, drives, concrete pads or walkways, compacted surfaces for driving, etc.","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"1f9948d0-b2b7-4f83-ba60-9873272864f2","uuid":"3c391c5b-3034-40e3-b21d-6343c598d313","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"/Users/wnavey/code/controlroom/conductor/workspace/projects/63cead15-41f8-418c-b0ef-bd5c2b44719a/supplementary-docs/master-comment-report/12-water-quality-review.md:* U1: Comment remains. A concrete splash pad may help with erosion but it does not help diffuse the flow. Where feasible the underdrain pipe should discharge to a gravel trench in order to diffuse the flow and promote infiltration and recharge. If a gravel trench is not feasible, other options are shown in the City of Austin Standards 508S-16 through 20. If there is existing gravel please show it on the pond sheets. \n/Users/wnavey/code/controlroom/conductor/workspace/projects/63cead15-41f8-418c-b0ef-bd5c2b44719a/supplementary-docs/master-comment-report/04-drainage-engineering-review.md:* U1: Comment pending. We still need an analysis of the existing channel on the site. If you are planning to discharge to it, we will need to ensure it has capacity. Please note that your outfall is within an electrical easement. You will need to provide approval from the easements’ owner to do this. Please note the intent of this code is also to cause no adverse impact to your neighbors.\n/Users/wnavey/code/controlroom/conductor/workspace/projects/63cead15-41f8-418c-b0ef-bd5c2b44719a/supplementary-docs/