L BE WELL CONSOLIDATED USING PROPER MECHANICAL VIBRATION.\n    68→3. MINIMUM CONCRETE CLEAR COVER SHALL BE\n    69→IN SLABS:\n    70→3/4\" ADJACENT TO INTERIOR SLAB SURFACES\n    71→1 1/2\" ABOVE SUB-GRADE\n    72→IN STIFFENING BEAMS, WALLS AND FOOTINGS:\n    73→1 1/2\" ADJACENT TO EXTERIOR OR INTERIOR SURFACES\n    74→3\" FROM SURFACES IN CONTACT WITH EARTH\n    75→AT PIPE PENETRATION IN BEAM OR SLAB:\n    76→2\" FOR REBAR\n    77→REINFORCEMENT:\n    78→1. REINFORCING STEEL TO BE GRADE 60 FOR #4 AND LARGER AND GRADE 40 FOR #3.\n    79→2. ADD 24\"x24\" CORNER BARS EQUAL TO SIZE AND SPACING OF DISCONTINUOUS HORIZ. STEEL.\n    80→\n    81→## Block 6: notes — A general note regarding the purpose of the schematic plan, confirming it is for locating and identifying foundation reinforcing elements only, and emphasizing the architect's responsibility for dimensional control.\n    82→\n    83→NOTE:\n    84→THIS IS A SCHEMATIC PLAN FOR THE PURPOSE OF LOCATING AND IDENTIFYING FOUNDATION REINFORCING ELEMENTS ONLY. VERIFY ALL DIMENSIONS, DROPS, OFFSETS AND FEATURES WITH THE ARCHITECTURAL PLANS BEFORE FORMING THE FOUNDATION. MLAW CANNOT BE HELD LIABLE FOR ANY CONTRACTOR OVERSIGHT IN THIS REGARD. DO NOT FORM FOUNDATION USING THESE PLANS. DIMENSIONAL CONTROL IS THE RESPONSIBILITY OF THE ARCHITECT. USE THESE PLANS FOR THE PLACEMENT OF THE GRADE BEAMS AND REINFORCEMENT.\n    85→\n    86→## Block 9: table — A table outlining the design criteria for the project, including allowable soil bearing, lateral pressure method, active soil pressure for the heel, passive pressure, and soil weight.\n    87→\n    88→DESIGN CRITERIA:\n    89→REQUIREMENTS OF IBC 2015\n    90→| *ALLOWABLE SOIL BEARING | : 4,000 PSF |\n    91→|---|---|\n    92→| *LATERAL PRESSURE METHOD | : E.F.P. |\n    93→| *ACTIVE SOIL PRESSURE - HEEL | : 45.5 PSF |\n    94→| *PASSIVE PRESSURE | : 290 PSF |\n    95→| *SOIL WEIGHT | : 130 PSF |\n    96→\n    97→## Block 10: table — A list of required inspections prior to specific construction phases.\n    98→\n    99→REQUIRED INSPECTIONS:\n   100→1. PRIOR TO CONCRETE PLACEMENT OF FOOTINGS.\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"18361ddd-02f6-4450-868d-9cbeabd274bb","uuid":"1a7c3cb3-c4ee-4115-af7c-27ce77bf8b5f","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/wnavey/code/controlroom/conductor/workspace/projects/63cead15-41f8-418c-b0ef-bd5c2b44719a/primary-site-plan/sheet-38/blocks.md","content":"# Sheet 38 — Content Blocks\n\n## Block 1: diagram — Elevation view drawings of a typical step in a footing, showing dimensions and requirements for the bottom of strip footing to be level, stepping bottom of footing as required.\n\n2'-0\"\nAS REQUIRED\n#4 @ 12\" O.C.E.W.\nBOTTOM OF STRIP FOOTING TO BE LEVEL, STEP BOTTOM OF FOOTING AS REQ'D.\n2'-0\"\n12\"\nSEE \"RETAINING WALL\" DETAILS FOR FOOTING REINFORCING\nELEVATION VIEW\nTYPICAL STEP IN FOOTING\n\n## Block 2: diagram — A cross-section detail showing how to install a new slab adjacent to an existing slab using a #6 rebar dowel. It includes instructions for cleaning the surface, drilling holes, and inserting the rebar with epoxy.\n\nEXISTING SLAB\nCLEAN EXISTING SURFACE PRIOR TO SETTING \"NEW\" CONCRETE\nNEW SLAB\n#6 REBAR (NOT SMOOTH DOWEL)\nDRILL HOLE WITH 7/8\" BIT WHEN USING #4 REBAR\nCLEAR HOLE WITH COMPRESSED AIR PRIOR TO INSERTING EPOXY.\n6\" EMBED\nDRILLED HOLE IN EXISTING CONCRETE SHOULD BE SIZED TO ALLOW REBAR INSTALLATION BY HAND. ONLY LIGHT \"TAPPING\" WITH HAMMER SHOULD BE REQUIRED.\nDOWEL DETAIL\n\n## Block 3: diagram — A cross-section detail of a retaining wall with a maximum height of 14'-0\". It includes specifications for rebar placement, gravel backfill, geotextile fabric, weep pipes, and dimensions for the wall and footing.\n\nCHAMFER EXPOSED EDGES (TYPICAL)\nEXISTING WALL\nGRAVEL BACK FILL\n#7 @ 10\" O.C. VERT.\n14'-0\" MAX.\nMIRAFI 140N GEOTEXTILE OR APPROVED EQUAL\n#4 @ 12\" O.C. VERT.\n#3 @ 12\" O.C. HORIZ. EACH FACE\nADD 2\" DIA. PVC WEEP PIPE @ 5' O.C. 8\" ABOVE WALL FOOTING \"TOE\"\nTOP OF GRADE\n#7 @ 10\" O.C. TOP AND BOTTOM\nADD (2)#6 DOWELS @ 18\" O.C. TOP AND BOTTOM SEE DETAIL THIS PAGE.\n#4 @ 12\" O.C. CONT. AS SHOWN\n14'-0\" MAX. RETAINING WALL\n\n## Block 4: drawing — A plan view drawing of retaining walls, pond walls, and existing walls. It includes callouts for a new retaining wall to be in place prior to demo for pond walls, an existing buttress with instructions to drill and epoxy #6 x 12\" dowels 6\" into the buttress 12\" O.C. tie to wall steel, and a callout to start on the south side of existing wall.\n\nPOND WALL REF PG 2\nPOND WALL REF PG 2\nPOND WALL REF PG 2\nPOND WALL REF PG 2\nNEW WALL TO BE IN PLACE PRIOR TO DEMO FOR POND WALLS.\nNEW RETAINING WALL REF DETAIL THIS PAGE\nEXISTING WALL\nEXISTING BUTTRESS DRILL AND EPOXY #6 X 12\" DOWELS 6\" INTO BUTTRESS 12\" O.C. TIE TO WALL STEEL\nNEW RETAINING WALL REF DETAIL THIS PAGE\nPLAN VIEW\nONE 20' SECTION OF NEW FOOTING TO BE INSTALLED AT A TIME. START AT THE SOUTH SIDE OF EXISTING WALL.\n\n## Block 5: notes — A list of notes covering general contractor responsibilities, concrete specifications, and reinforcement requirements.\n\nNOTES:\n1. THE CONTRACTOR IS RESPONSIBLE FOR OBTAINING ALL PERMITS NECESSARY TO LEGALLY COMMENCE WITH THIS WORK.\n2. OBTAIN LOCATION AND DEPTH OF EXISTING UTILITY LINES PRIOR TO BEGINNING CONSTRUCTION.\n3. CONTRACTOR SHALL DISPOSE OF ALL SPOILS LEGALLY AT AN OFFSITE LOCATION OR AS DIRECTED BY OWNER.\n4. SHOULD CONDITIONS ARISE THAT ARE NOT COVERED BY DETAILS ON THIS PLAN, CONTACT ENGINEER AT ONCE FOR ADDITIONAL INSTRUCTIONS.\nCONCRETE:\n1. CONCRETE TO HAVE A MINIMUM 28 DAY COMPRESSIVE STRENGTH OF 3000 PSI.\n2. CONCRETE SHALL BE WELL CONSOLIDATED USING PROPER MECHANICAL VIBRATION.\n3. MINIMUM CONCRETE CLEAR COVER SHALL BE\nIN SLABS:\n3/4\" ADJACENT TO INTERIOR SLAB SURFACES\n1 1/2\" ABOVE SUB-GRADE\nIN STIFFENING BEAMS, WALLS AND FOOTINGS:\n1 1/2\" ADJACENT TO EXTERIOR OR INTERIOR SURFACES\n3\" FROM SURFACES IN CONTACT WITH EARTH\nAT PIPE PENETRATION IN BEAM OR SLAB:\n2\" FOR REBAR\nREINFORCEMENT:\n1. REINFORCING STEEL TO BE GRADE 60 FOR #4 AND LARGER AND GRADE 40 FOR #3.\n2. ADD 24\"x24\" CORNER BARS EQUAL TO SIZE AND SPACING OF DISCONTINUOUS HORIZ. STEEL.\n\n## Block 6: notes — A general note regarding the purpose of the schematic plan, confirming it is for locating and identifying foundation reinforcing elements only, and emphasizing the architect's responsibility for dimensional control.\n\nNOTE:\nTHIS IS A SCHEMATIC PLAN FOR THE PURPOSE OF LOCATING AND IDENTIFYING FOUNDATION REINFORCING ELEMENTS ONLY. VERIFY ALL DIMENSIONS, DROPS, OFFSETS AND FEATURES WITH THE ARCHITECTURAL PLANS BEFORE FORMING THE FOUNDATION. MLAW CANNOT BE HELD LIABLE FOR ANY CONTRACTOR OVERSIGHT IN THIS REGARD. DO NOT FORM FOUNDATION USING THESE PLANS. DIMENSIONAL CONTROL IS THE RESPONSIBILITY OF THE ARCHITECT. USE THESE PLANS FOR THE PLACEMENT OF THE GRADE BEAMS AND REINFORCEMENT.\n\n## Block 9: table — A table outlining the design criteria for the project, including allowable soil bearing, lateral pressure method, active soil pressure for the heel, passive pressure, and soil weight.\n\nDESIGN CRITERIA:\nREQUIREMENTS OF IBC 2015\n| *ALLOWABLE SOIL BEARING | : 4,000 PSF |\n|---|---|\n| *LATERAL PRESSURE METHOD | : E.F.P. |\n| *ACTIVE SOIL PRESSURE - HEEL | : 45.5 PSF |\n| *PASSIVE PRESSURE | : 290 PSF |\n| *SOIL WEIGHT | : 130 PSF |\n\n## Block 10: table — A list of required inspections prior to specific construction phases.\n\nREQUIRED INSPECTIONS:\n1. PRIOR TO CONCRETE PLACEMENT OF FOOTINGS.","numLines":100,"startLine":1,"totalLines":104}}}
{"level":30,"time":1778523585146,"pid":86444,"hostname":"Williams-MacBook-Pro.local","step":"review-runs","item":"1.md","index":3,"runIndex":"run-1","type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001","id":"msg_011aGTxYgdYrawhnEYgLhV8K","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The retaining wall details (Sheet 38) show typical retaining wall construction details but there's no mention of overhead electric line clearances. The notes mention obtaining location of existing utility lines but don't specifically address overhead electric clearances.\n\nNow I have enough information to systematically evaluate all 47 checklist items. Let me organize my findings and create the output. \n\n**SUMMARY OF KEY FINDINGS:**\n\n**Applicability:**\nThe grouping IS APPLICABLE because:\n1. Overhead electric lines exist on and adjacent to the property (confirmed by vision check of Sheet 4)\n2. Buildings 1, 2, 7, and 8 are located near the overhead lines\n3. Proposed structures, fences, retaining walls, and landscaping will be within the site\n\n**Critical Deficiencies Identified:**\n\n1. **No Clearance Dimensions Shown on Plans**: While measured distances from the buildings to overhead lines exceed minimum clearances, these distances are NOT dimensioned on the site plans\n\n2. **No Building Elevation Sheets with Surveyed Conductor Elevations**: No dedicated elevation sheets provided showing the surveyed elevation of overhead conductors and vertical clearances to buildings\n\n3. **No 48-Hour Notification Note**: The required note per Texas Health & Safety Code Chapter 752, Section 752.003 is not included on the plans\n\n4. **Lack of Tree Utility Compatibility Verification**: Proposed trees (Cedar Elm, Live Oak, Mexican Redbud, Texas Mountain Laurel) are not verified against ECM Appendix F Utility Compatible list\n\n5. **No Clearance Verification for Driveways/Parking**: No dimension annotations showing that driveways and parking areas maintain required 16-18 foot vertical clearances from overhead lines\n\nNow let me evaluate each of the 47 checklist items systematically. The grouping applies to this project because overhead electric facilities exist on/adjacent to the site and buildings/structures are proposed. However, based on my research, I need to determine which items are:\n\n\n- PASS: Requirement is met\n- FAIL: Requirement is not met (deficiency is present)\n- NOT-VERIFIABLE: Cannot determine from available documents\n- N/A: Requirement does not apply\n\nLet me start evaluating:\n\n**EL-1.1: Buildings and permanent structures do not maintain minimum 7 feet 6 inches horizontal sky-to-ground clearance from overhead electric conductors**\n- Applicability: YES - Buildings 1, 2, 7, 8 are near overhead lines\n- Evidence: Measured distances from vision check show ~78-86 feet for west lines and ~27.8 feet for south lines\n- Issue: Distances are MEASURED but not SHOWN with dimensions on the plans, so while the clearances appear adequate, they're not documented or dimensioned for verification purposes\n\n. The measured distances of 27-86 feet clearly exceed the 7.5-foot requirement, making this a PASS despite the documentation gap.\n\n**EL-1.2: Customer facilities do not maintain minimum 15-foot radial clearance from outside conductor of overhead distribution primary and neutral conductors**\n- Applicability: YES - Buildings are customer facilities near overhead distribution lines\n- Evidence: Buildings 1, 2, 7, 8 are 27-86 feet from overhead lines, which exceeds 15-foot radial requirement\n- Status: PASS\n\n**EL-1.3: Building proj\n\nections (overhangs, awnings, balconies, decks, roofs, patios) do not maintain required clearances - no section views or elevation sheets provided to verify building projections against overhead conductor clearances, so this cannot be confirmed as compliant. **EL-1.4: Gas pump canopies do not maintain required clearances from overhead electric lines** - not applicable since no gas pump canopies appear on the site plan. **EL-1.5: Stairs, elevated walkways, and elevated platforms do not maintain minimum 15-foot radial clearance** - the site plan shows walkways and circulation but it's unclear whether any elevated structures are actually proposed, so this needs closer examination though it appears to be not applicable.\n\nFor the overhead lines along Valley View Road, the buildings do appear positioned far enough away based on measured distances, though the plans lack dimension annotations to formally document this clearance. The bigger issue is that no dedicated elevation sheets exist showing the surveyed conductor elevations, which is required. Additionally, the section and plan views don't include the necessary details showing how building elements relate to the overhead electric facilities.\n\nThe site and grading plans are missing dimension annotations that would show the clearance distances from the outer conductors to the building edges and any projections. For the fences shown on the landscape and site plans, particularly those along the western and southern property lines where the overhead lines run, it's unclear whether they maintain the required 7.5-foot clearance since no clearance dimensions are provided and the relationship between fence locations and conductor positions isn't documented.\n\nGiven that the overhead lines follow Valley View Road on the west side and the southern boundary, property line fences at these locations would be positioned directly beneath or extremely close to the overhead conductors—essentially at zero feet from the line. This fails to meet the 7.5-foot clearance requirement. Fence posts also lack documented clearances from the utility poles marked on the survey and site plan, making it impossible to verify the required 10-foot horizontal separation without dimension annotations.\n\nNo Austin Energy substation appears on the site plan, so electrical isolation requirements don't apply. Regarding transmission line fencing standards, the overhead lines appear to be distribution infrastructure rather than transmission lines—the site has electric easements typical of distribution systems operating below 60kV, whereas transmission operates at 69kV or higher. Since voltage levels aren't specified and the evidence points to distribution service, transmission line fencing standards don't apply.\n\nVehicle barrier fencing beneath overhead lines isn't shown on the plans, so clearance requirements for that element are moot. However, retaining walls are present near the detention pond on the south side where overhead lines run along the boundary. The grading plan shows these wall locations but provides no dimension annotations indicating clearances from the overhead lines, making it impossible to verify compliance with the 7.5-foot horizontal clearance requirement.\n\nFor the Austin Energy substation separation requirement, no substation appears on the site plans, so that standard doesn't apply. Similarly, no emergency access structures like elevated platforms or bridges are indicated, so those clearance standards are also not applicable.\n\nThe retaining wall section details on Sheet 38 do show dimensions, but they're missing the critical clearance measurements from the overhead conductors to the wall's outer edge—this is a failure. Driveways and parking areas are present throughout the site, yet there are no vertical clearance annotations indicating whether they meet the 16-foot minimum (for standard parking) or 18-foot minimum (for truck traffic areas) from the overhead lines to the ground surface. Vehicle parking and display areas also need to be evaluated for clearance compliance.\n\nRegarding transmission lines, the overhead facilities appear to be distribution-level rather than transmission-level (≥60kV), so the 30-foot clearance requirement doesn't apply here. There are no highways or freeways on the property, eliminating that standard. However, existing utility poles do appear to conflict with the proposed driveway layout and haven't been relocated the required 30 feet from the driveway edge.\n\nFor the clearance measurement standard, no dimension annotations are provided on the plans, making it impossible to verify whether measurements are taken from the conductor or pole centerline.\n\nFor the streetlight requirement, the site plan specifies full cut-off and fully shielded lighting fixtures on buildings, but it's unclear whether standalone streetlight poles are part of the design. The applicability cannot be determined from available documentation.\n\nFor sidewalk clearances, sidewalks are proposed along Valley View Road with existing utility poles present, but there are no clearance annotations to verify adequate spacing. For trash areas, they're shown on the site plan but lack documentation confirming proper clearance from overhead electric facilities.\n\nRegarding customer facilities and electric easements, buildings and structures are positioned 27-86 feet from overhead lines, which appears adequate. However, the property includes 5-foot electric easements where any proposed improvements must maintain at least 7.5 feet of clearance from the outer conductor. The critical question is whether any structures or improvements fall within these marked easement boundaries, as this would trigger the clearance requirement regardless of the easement width.\n\nFor water meters, the utility plans identify their locations but don't provide clearance dimensions relative to existing overhead service drops. Without documented measurements showing the 7.5-foot horizontal sky-to-ground clearance, this cannot be verified. Similarly, water line routing and clearances from overhead electric facilities are not dimensioned on the plans, making verification impossible at this stage.\n\nBuilding setbacks appear adequate—structures 1, 2, 7, and 8 are positioned 27+ feet from property lines where overhead lines exist, which should provide sufficient clearance space for electric infrastructure. However, the survey documentation showing pole locations and mid-span clearances is missing, which prevents verification of vertical clearances to building roofs and driveways. Building finish floor elevations are documented (ranging from 685.50 to 687.50), but overhead conductor elevations aren't provided, making it impossible to confirm whether the required 7.5-foot horizontal and 16-18 foot vertical clearances are actually met. The orientation of overhead line crossings relative to Valley View Road also remains uncertain and unverified.\n\nSecondary and primary conductor hierarchy isn't documented, so I can't determine if one set of lines crosses over another. Marquee signage isn't part of this proposal. No new poles for underground service conversion appear on the plans. Decorative lighting may exist within the landscape design but clearance compliance under existing overhead lines cannot be confirmed.\n\nTree species selection is a concern—Cedar Elm, Live Oak, Mexican Redbud, and Texas Mountain Laurel are proposed, yet I haven't verified whether they're compatible with utility corridors per ECM Appendix F standards. Trees within 10 feet laterally of distribution lines or 50 feet of transmission lines must come from the approved list, and this verification is missing. Electrical meter placement is shown on the site plan, though I need to confirm no obstructions from structures or landscaping block access.\n\nRegarding streetlight clearances, existing fixtures along Valley View Road may be present, but documentation of their locations and confirmation that new construction maintains proper distances isn't evident. Down guy wires from utility poles could conflict with accessible routes or driveways, yet their positions aren't documented on the plans—the grouping guide notes that electrical infrastructure is frequently excluded from submissions.\n\nGuy easements for down guy anchors appear limited to transmission angled guys rather than distribution down guys. Down guy anchor points may require dedicated easements depending on whether such guys are actually present and what standards apply.\n\nFor transformers, the electrical design shows units on pads, but vertical clearance dimensions from overhead lines aren't provided. The overhead lines appear along the west and south boundaries while transformers seem positioned internally, suggesting adequate separation.\n\nNiche service configurations aren't indicated on the plans. Construction staging areas are shown but lack clearance notations relative to the overhead lines that run near the site boundaries.\n\nThe plans are missing the required note instructing the owner and contractor to contact Austin Energy at least 48 hours before commencing work near high-voltage overhead lines. Service-mast clearances cannot be verified since building elevation sheets weren't provided with the submission.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":7,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2375,"cache_read_input_tokens":69353,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2375},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"18361ddd-02f6-4450-868d-9cbeabd274bb","uuid":"83ae87b9-a181-493b-b657-958a57e641f6"}
